+one hard link. Every pathname component is opened without following symlinks.
+Ancestors must be root-owned or owned by the invoking uid and not writable by
+group/other, except root-owned sticky ancestors such as `/private/tmp`. The
+final parent must be owned by the invoking uid and not writable by group/other.
+Literal symlink aliases such as macOS `/tmp` are refused; use `/private/tmp`.
+Input/output operations retain their anchored parent fd. Output cleanup uses
+`unlinkat` only after comparing the created inode. The threat model excludes a
+malicious process already running as the same uid, which can already read the
+private inputs and session credentials. Home access applies the same ancestor
+rules and retains directory descriptors while the pathname-based runtime API
+opens the database. Its exact request fields are:
 
